Backport of qtbase 6192d9edd00caa14ed6b67c32c0cd8cfe95cf815 (in Qt >= 6.12.0):
"macOS: Don't assume the current event is a mouse event in the tray icon".

On macOS 27, NSControls are driven by gesture recognizers, so by the time the
status item's action / NSMenuDidBeginTrackingNotification fires,
NSApp.currentEvent can be e.g. a MouseExited event. Asking it for
clickCount raises NSInternalInconsistencyException and aborts the app
(seafile-applet crashes as soon as the tray menu is opened).

Upstream adds a qt_mac_isMouseEvent() helper that doesn't exist in 6.11.2;
this uses the existing cocoaEvent2QtMouseEvent() it wraps instead.
Drop this once nixpkgs ships Qt >= 6.12.0.

--- a/src/plugins/platforms/cocoa/qcocoasystemtrayicon.mm
+++ b/src/plugins/platforms/cocoa/qcocoasystemtrayicon.mm
@@ -244,6 +244,15 @@
 
 void QCocoaSystemTrayIcon::emitActivated()
 {
+    // NSControls are driven by gesture recognizers in macOS 27,
+    // which means NSApp.currentEvent may not be a mouse event
+    // at the point when we receive the status bar button's
+    // action callback. This is a quick fix to avoid crashing.
+    if (cocoaEvent2QtMouseEvent(NSApp.currentEvent) == QEvent::None) {
+        emit activated(QPlatformSystemTrayIcon::Unknown);
+        return;
+    }
+
     auto *mouseEvent = NSApp.currentEvent;
 
     auto activationReason = QPlatformSystemTrayIcon::Unknown;
